(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["cs-easy-2026-013"]={"v":1,"id":"cs-easy-2026-013","title":"Mock Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","sourceTitle":"Mock Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS","branch":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING","code":"CS","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering","seriesId":"cs-easy-2026","num":13,"category":"mock","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Computer Science Engineering"},"source":"COMPUTER SCIENCE ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Computer_Science_Engineering/024_Mock Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 CS.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the below question followed by three statements. You have to study the question and the statements and decide which of the statement(s) is/are necessary to answer the given question.<br />\nA, B and C together start a business with a total investment of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da91251fe33dbf1471e7.png\" /> At the end of the year, the total profit is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/2211c20705f88f1f7e1e.png\" /> What is A’s share in the profit?<br />\n1. A’s contribution is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/571a28b567c9698614a9.png\" /> times B’s contribution.<br />\n2. B’s contribution is twice that of C.<br />\n3. A’s contribution is thrice that of C.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"B","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"C","html":"All 1, 2 and 3"},{"key":"D","html":"Any two of the three"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/91a49c0b45964e671d3e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Find the next term in the alpha-numerical series:<br />\nZ1A, X2D, V6G, T21J, R88M, P445P, ?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"N2676S"},{"key":"B","html":"N2676T"},{"key":"C","html":"T26TON"},{"key":"D","html":"T2676N"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c24006aada2079c59084.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Of the five villages P, Q, R, S and T situated close to each other, P is 1 m to the west of Q, R is 1 m to the south of P, T is 1 m to the north of Q and S is 1 m to the east of T. Then R is in which direction with respect to S?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"North-West"},{"key":"B","html":"South-East"},{"key":"C","html":"South-West"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d80606359c36474cd08a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A conical vessel whose internal radius is 10 cm and height 72 cm is full of water. If this water is poured into a cylindrical vessel with internal radius 30 cm, the height of the water level rises in it is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f8d9c4280fd58a7bb01.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/817475010e991c937c59.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/56/563cf7cb19a824372cbd.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6be0b3a7b62e983f7a1f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>An opaque solid cone is suspendended in the path of a parallel beam of light such that its shadow is cast on a screen oriented perpendicular to the direction of the light beam.<br />\nUnder these conditions, which one of the shadows <em>A</em>, <em>B</em>, <em>C</em> and <em>D</em> is not possible?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3c74dd8bf2da97a6b6f5.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A"},{"key":"B","html":"B"},{"key":"C","html":"C"},{"key":"D","html":"D"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n&bull; A cone&rsquo;s circular base can project a circle when the cone&rsquo;s axis is perpendicular to the screen.<br />\n&bull; Tilt the cone a bit and the circle becomes an ellipse.<br />\n&bull; If the cone is oriented so the apex and base edge project on the screen, the outline can form a<br />\ntriangle C the lateral surface projects as two straight sloping edges meeting at the apex.</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The sum of all the numbers greater than 10000 formed by using digits 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 (no digit repeated in any number) is equal to?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"5199960"},{"key":"B","html":"5209960"},{"key":"C","html":"5199980"},{"key":"D","html":"5299960"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/16b716747e3f8daa98d3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":7,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Monica has a piece of canvas whose area is 551 m<sup>2</sup>. She uses it to have a conical tent made, with a base radius of 7 m. Assuming that all the stitching margins and the wastage incurred while cutting, amounts to approximately 1 m2, the volume of the tent is _____ m<sup>3</sup>.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1225,1239],"answerText":"1232","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1232 (1225 - 1239)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5d/5d44770e139a6e3f0d8b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The diameter of a sphere is decreased by 25%. By what percentage does its curved surface area decrease?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"25%"},{"key":"B","html":"37.5%"},{"key":"C","html":"43.75%"},{"key":"D","html":"50%"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nIf the diameter decreases by 25%, the radius also decreases by 25%.<br />\nSo the new radius = 75% of the old radius = 0.75r<br />\nCurved surface area is proportional to the square of the radius : (0.75)<sup>2</sup> = 0.5625<br />\nThis means the new area is 56.25% of the original area<br />\nSo, the decrease = 100 &ndash; 56.25 = 43.75%<br />\nTherefore, the curved surface area decreases by 43.75%.</p>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A tariff against computers made in country z is needed to protect computer manufactures in this country. With such a tariff, domestic computer manufactures would see increased sales of their own products. And increased sales frequently lead to reduced prices. The conclusion which can<br />\nbe most properly drawn from above statement is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A lack of protective tariff has hampered computer production in this country."},{"key":"B","html":"A tariff would probably lead to a drop in prices for buyers of domestically produced computers."},{"key":"C","html":"Domestic manufacturers will decrease computer production if a protective tariff is not established"},{"key":"D","html":"A drop in computer prices is likely to create more jobs in the country."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"NAT","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0","html":"<p>All the soldier are arranged in the form of an equivalent triangle i.e. one soldier in front row, 2 soldier in second row and 3 soldier in third row and so on. If 669 more soldiers from other are added in such a way that all the soldiers form a square and each of the side contains 8 less soldier<br />\nthan each side of equilateral triangle. The number of soldier initially were _________ .</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1540,1540],"answerText":"1540","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1540</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bb4ebe47b4757cc09620.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the relation ‘<em>R</em>’ on the power set <em>P</em>(<em>A</em>) of a set A as,</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7d/7d167a3cc54495186c06.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is true?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>R</em> is not reflexive, not transitive but symmetric"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>R</em> is reflexive, transitive but not symmetric"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>R</em> is reflexive, symmetric and transitive"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>R</em> is reflexive, not symmetric, not transitive"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6fb091058c158528661b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following regular expressions are equivalent to the grammar G given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73125da858d0e4beb5c4.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(1* 01* 0)* 1"},{"key":"B","html":"1* (01* 01*)*"},{"key":"C","html":"(1* 01* 01*)* + 1"},{"key":"D","html":"(1* 01* 0)* + 1*"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Suppose that a ‘x’ is a continuous random variable whose probability density function in given</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/77bed8f5f6bc1f6d4690.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>then the value of ‘<em>c</em>’ is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74d1f8e4e2bf3b11c85f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0a/0a31eee2ef7de70cf375.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6fb7534d839e481b8613.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"1"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3a/3ae845f475b86dc7d612.png\" /></div>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The two inputs <em>A</em> and <em>B</em> are connected to an <em>R</em> – <em>S</em> latch via two AND gates as shown in the figure.<br />\nIf <em>A</em> = 1 and <em>B</em> = 0, the output <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/6a/6aed2c1ca2928877e015.png\" /> is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/139bff7798793d01b03c.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"00"},{"key":"B","html":"10"},{"key":"C","html":"01"},{"key":"D","html":"11"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nIt is JK configuration with <em>J</em> = <em>A</em>, <em>K</em> = <em>B</em>.<br />\n<em>J</em> = 1, <em>K</em> = 0 &rarr; set condition</p>"},{"n":15,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of following factors prevent it from occupying the entire bandwidth, even though UDP has no congestion control?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Application layer flow control"},{"key":"B","html":"Router queue capacity"},{"key":"C","html":"Physical link bandwidth"},{"key":"D","html":"TCP congestion avoidance"}],"answer":["B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c)<br />\nRouter queues are finite, and number of packets processed by router per second is finite.</p>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following statement is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Synchronization is always achieved when semaphore used."},{"key":"B","html":"If virtual memory is removed from system, then multi-programming is implemented efficiently."},{"key":"C","html":"Dissatisfying hold and wait in deadlock prevention not suffer starvation for the processes."},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":17,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following directed graph G(V, E):</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/eafe74eaa343db13f1d1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Apply depth-first-search (DFS) on G with node 1 as start node, then there are more than one DFS tree possible. Minimum number of cross edges in a DFS tree (obtained above) is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1,1],"answerText":"1","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/49/493ae62d17b24752b92f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6d1eabe2a02159450e9f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following expression:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e869e5824cba57f5dd8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The number of nodes in directed acyclic graph is x and edges is y, then value of 2<em>x</em> – y is ________?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/16f027fa03defd322491.png\" /></div>"},{"n":19,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following graph G:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7b1272cd8d9c4c463e6d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Find the number of Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) is possible?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/71e86e24accb69b1f464.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider following relational schema:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/dd131aaa4448810f4d46.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Query1 only"},{"key":"B","html":"Query 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"Both Query1 and Query 2"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3ca11ae067e3727bed48.png\" /></div>"},{"n":21,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following C program:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8f/8fe9a92b7e7f9efc99e8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The output of above program on execution is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[17,17],"answerText":"17","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>17</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/39010bd914ba532a5992.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Every finite lattice is bounded."},{"key":"B","html":"If a relation is symmetric then its transitive closure is also symmetric."},{"key":"C","html":"In a lattice an element can not have more than one complement."},{"key":"D","html":"In a distributive lattice if complement of an element exist then it is unique."}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)<br />\nIn a lattice an element can have more than one complement also.</p>"},{"n":23,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A logic circuit implements the Boolean function <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bf18ee8d89d2caa310b4.png\" /> It is found that the input combination <em>x</em> = y = 1 can never occur. Taking this into accounts a simplified expression for F is given by</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/64f2c19ecc47624448b8.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d2/d21970042a76e553f1d4.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3daea7c769e3a3dbb53c.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/99/998212b57dcef7042eda.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D, A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/11/110f452cc1fe343c3e8c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A bottleneck link has total available bandwidth “B”. If K long lived TCP connections share this link fairly, what is the approximate bandwidth each TCP connection receives?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"KB"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/18fd3051406b321d958a.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/42/42e261c8d38dab513893.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following six disk access requests of the form (request id, cylinder number) that are present in the disk scheduler queue at a given time.<br />\n(P, 42), (Q, 239), (R, 30), (S, 150), (T, 180), (U, 120)<br />\nAssume the head is positioned at cylinder 100. The scheduler follows shortest seek time first scheduling to service the requests.<br />\nWhich one of the following statement is false?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"T is serviced before P"},{"key":"B","html":"Q is serviced before R"},{"key":"C","html":"R is serviced after P but before S"},{"key":"D","html":"The head reverse its direction of movement between servicing of Q and P"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c63757e7190423b82c32.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":26,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The number of rows in the truth table for proposition <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d78b34b149f77882aa44.png\" /> which evaluate to true is equal to ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>12</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da4f307c0ffe4611f373.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Randomized exponential back-off is a mechanism used to stabilize contention MAC protocols.<br />\nWhich of the following statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"It ensures that two nodes that experience a collision in a time-slot will never collide with each other when they each retry that packet."},{"key":"B","html":"It ensures that two or more nodes that experience a collision in a time-slot will experience a lower probability of colliding with each other when they each retry that packet."},{"key":"C","html":"It can be used with slotted Aloha but not with CSMA."},{"key":"D","html":"It can be used with CSMA but not with slotted Aloha."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, A, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n(a) False, they might get unlucky and back-off the same amount.<br />\n(b) True, the probability of a repeat collision is a smaller in each subsequent backoff.<br />\n(c) False, it can be used in any contention period.<br />\n(d) False, it can be used in any contention period.</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following instances of job sequencing problem, where in we have 5 jobs <em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em>, <em>S </em>and <em>T</em> with their associated profits and deadline tabulated below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/38/380d8cf1d74e372cba1b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following jobs are not include in the optimal solution?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>P</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>R</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>T</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>Q</em>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9a/9a31a91812aafc4b8ad1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a system with 2 way set-associative cache of 16 cache blocks and uses LRU replacement algorithm. Assume cache is initially empty. A process accesses following memory blocks in order:<br />\n0, 5, 31, 8, 45, 16, 5, 8, 0, 29, 5, 39, 15, 45, 8, 31<br />\nWhich of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3 conflict misses experienced by cache."},{"key":"B","html":"5 hits experienced by cache."},{"key":"C","html":"Block 8 never removed from cache block due to any miss."},{"key":"D","html":"Block 15 and 45 exist in cache after all requests and in same set."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>"},{"n":30,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following problems are undecidable?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Does a given program ever produce an output?"},{"key":"B","html":"If <em>L</em> is a context-free language, then, is ~ <em>L</em> also context-free?"},{"key":"C","html":"If <em>L</em> is a regular language, then, is ~ <em>L</em> also regular?"},{"key":"D","html":"If <em>L</em> is a recursive language, then, is ~ <em>L</em> also recursive"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)<br />\n(a) Equivalent to the Halting Problem. It is undecidable because we cannot predict in general<br />\nwhether a program will halt and produce output.<br />\n(b) CFLs are not closed under complement, and there is no algorithm to decide if the complement<br />\nof a given CFL is also CFL hence, this problem is undecidable.<br />\n(c) Regular languages are closed under complement, and we can construct the complement DFA<br />\nfrom a given DFA. This problem is decidable.<br />\n(d) Recursive languages are closed under complement, so membership in I can always be decided.<br />\nThis problem is decidable.</p>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A number <em>N</em> is stored in a 4 bit 2’s complement representation as</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/25/258a912a1cd29b7d7883.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The value of this bit pattern in 2’s complement representation in terms of the original number <em>N</em> as</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"32<em>a</em><sub>3</sub> + 2<em>N</em> + 1"},{"key":"B","html":"32<em>a</em><sub>3</sub> – 2<em>N</em> – 1"},{"key":"C","html":"2<em>N</em> – 1"},{"key":"D","html":"2<em>N</em> + 1"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/96bfe63f012d96ce57cf.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a binary min heap tree T in array with following elements from start of array:<br />\n7, 12, 17, 21, 15, 18, 19, 27, 22, 20, 24<br />\nInsert key 16 and then remove min element of heap. Which of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Root of heap after first operation (insert 16) is 7."},{"key":"B","html":"Root of heap after both operations is 12."},{"key":"C","html":"Preorder traversal of heap tree after both operations is 12, 15, 16, 21, 18, 17, 19, 27, 22, 20, 24."},{"key":"D","html":"After both operations 18 is left child of 15."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cb/cb0db662bb375c9fe726.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/78/78535c456fa22ac590e1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a system allocate fixed four frames to a process P. Process P references following pages in given sequence:<br />\n1, 5, 3, 8, 3, 6, 5, 1, 7, 8, 3, 5, 1, 3, 5<br />\nLet <em>n</em> and <em>m</em> are number of page faults occurs for First-In-First-Out (FIFO) and Least-Recently- Used (LRU) page replacement policies respectively used by system on execution of P as shown above. The value of n + m is ________. (Initially frames are empty)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[20,20],"answerText":"20","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>20</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74db6475ce1b5ca9b8be.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a disk drive with the following specifications 16 surface, 512 tracks/surface, 512 sectors/track, 1 KB/sector, rotation speed 3000 rpm. The disk is operated in cycle stealing mode where by when ever two byte word is ready. It is sent to memory; memory cycle time is 40 μs. The maximum percentage of time that the CPU gets blocked during DMA operations is ________.<br />\n(Upto 1 decimal place)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[51,51.8],"answerText":"51.2","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>51.2 [51.0 - 51.8]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/45f1bfd61cf76e6b1c7d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following languages:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/0487c8f3b49a68a1c99c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Out of the above the number of languages which are regular is/are ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1,1],"answerText":"1","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b3e33703d386b48dcce5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>G</em> be a group whose identity elements is <em>e</em>. Assume that <em>g</em>, <em>h</em> ∈ <em>G</em> satisfy the following.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d6/d6e36841afc5d9b1158a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The conjugate of h by g equals <em>h</em><sup>2</sup> i.e., <em>ghg</em><sup>–1</sup> = <em>h</em><sup>2</sup>.<br />\nWhich of the following are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The order of <em>h</em> is 3"},{"key":"B","html":"The order of <em>h</em> is 4"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>h</em><sup>2</sup> = <em>e</em>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>h</em><sup>3</sup> = <em>e</em>"}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/41/41acd76395aeba50d14d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f0518b104bac0acdc6f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let R(A, B, C, D, E) be a relational schema with the following functional dependencies:<br />\nF = A → B, B → C, AD → C, B → A, EC → DB<br />\nF is set of above functional dependencies.<br />\nThen which of the following are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(BD)<sup>+</sup> = {A, B, C, D}"},{"key":"B","html":"D is extraneous attribute in AD → C"},{"key":"C","html":"F<sup>+</sup> – {B → C} is logically equivalent to F<sup>+</sup>, where F<sup>+</sup> is closure of F"},{"key":"D","html":"F logically implies EA → D"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a04336c22ba0526f52e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider 4-way set associative cache of 256 KB organized into a 16 word blocks. Word size is 64 bit. To which cache set in octal the following main memory addressed data is mapped (0xFCB8630)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"714"},{"key":"B","html":"516"},{"key":"C","html":"414"},{"key":"D","html":"280"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/ac5efdb536376c3d9077.png\" /></div>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Choose the appropriate context-free language <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> that ensure that <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> ∩ <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is NOT context free, where <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3c3f93dafd0f15c100cf.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/1005ddffe616573a1b2c.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e5/e539490555bcaf129aa5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/7765b9e599bbc248ada3.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/36/368849efccf313cacd34.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/75/75276d3994bee27457b6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>S</em> be a stack with standard operations push( ), pop( ) and <em>Q</em> be a queue with standard operations<br />\nenqueue( ) and dequeue( ). Assume, <em>S</em> and <em>Q</em> are initially empty. Apply following operations on <em>S</em> and <em>Q</em> in given sequence</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/10d8f879e39b2db1192f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The value of element at front position of <em>Q</em> is ________.<br />\n<strong>Note:</strong> Use front and rear pointers for deletion and insertion in queue.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f0/f0cd06797f2117a8882f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the grammar <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub> over {a, b}:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3c/3ca0361a88e5cda99e34.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Then the grammar <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub> corresponding to <em>L</em>(<em>G</em><sub>1</sub>) and <em>L</em>(<em>G</em><sub>2</sub>) respectively are</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f5/f554b280c4dcb3298af0.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f9/f97dbd8ff92779c750b1.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f358164b6b02f1e9886.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nNone of these is the correct answer.</p>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following relational schema Adj(x, y) which is used to state edges of its directed graph:<br />\nWhich of the following results vertices set which forms loop atmost two vertices?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a2/a24ea03da6791f9a821f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/5390674d21efe7db6643.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7f/7fb99653ebbf225334d8.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/69/6902385f2e7f04ab761b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":43,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A team is to be formed out of a committee consisting of 3 boys and 3 girls. Then the number of ways of teaming up the boys and girls such that there are an equal number of boys and girls in the team, assuming at least one boy and girl is selected, is equal to _________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[19,19],"answerText":"19","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>19</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b65c577a751f75f67cfd.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If the eigen vectors of a 2 × 2 matrix A corresponding to the eigen values –1 and 6 are <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/c0/c0f0b29265aa5c3887d3.png\" /> and <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/21/2123f13a9d75e0028438.png\" /> respectively, then the matrix <em>A</em> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/02/02872629eaef06f87cfb.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2be338382b14f0a9af38.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bd/bd53759b8bddbe84b5f7.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/24/2433538461b730fcefc3.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8e/8e62493053c5df28cfe4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Applications which can have reliable data transfer even when the application runs over UDP?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"TFTP"},{"key":"B","html":"VOIP"},{"key":"C","html":"HTTP"},{"key":"D","html":"SMTP"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)<br />\n&bull; TFTP uses sequence numbers at application layer.<br />\n&bull; VOIP uses sequence numbers and timestamp to reconstruct timing at the receiver.</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Let the clock cycles required for various operations be as follows:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5b5ad3754460f35a2023.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The total number of clock cycles required to execute the program is ________.</div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[76,76],"answerText":"76","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>76</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/704255ed92cd9307fe00.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following graph:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d0/d0ea8e11fe2af0df826e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which one of the following is not the sequence of edges added to the minimum spanning tree using prim’s algorithm?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9f9c97940e62126daa8f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/80f38a9bde31b317983f.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/05/05ff198f5813f1baa992.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/eb13ef0a3fe9f0465f4e.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ae/aefea3467773ac3e867e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Assuming that malloc is successful in each of these for allocating memory, select all options which are likely to create problems with memory i.e., runtime error or memory leak.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6d/6d120a630afea1ea979c.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Program 1"},{"key":"B","html":"Program 2"},{"key":"C","html":"Program 3"},{"key":"D","html":"Program 4"}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\n(a) Writing into freed memory is illegal. Dangling pointer.<br />\n(b) Here in the last line, the location of pointer is changed to 0 after it is set to NULL. This is valid<br />\nsince the value at the memory location pointed by NULL pointer is not accessed.<br />\n(c) Memory leak as the first int is not freed.<br />\n(d) This is illegal as memory assigned for char but int is assigned.</p>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following C program:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/349497c019591d46b1e2.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following type of error (earliest phase) is identified during compilation of the program?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Lexical error"},{"key":"B","html":"Syntax error"},{"key":"C","html":"Semantic error"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/ba71da5204dde11ab090.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of one of the following statements is FALSE for all positive functions <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>) = θ( <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>)<sup>2</sup>) where <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) is polynomial"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>) = ο( <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>)<sup>2</sup>)"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>) = Ο( <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>)<sup>2</sup>) where <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) is an exponential function"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>n</em><sup>2</sup>) = Ω( <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>)<sup>2</sup>)"}],"answer":["B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6b/6b1d1a984e7a3acb5ce6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>If f(x) = 12x<sup>5</sup> – 45x<sup>4</sup> – 20x<sup>3</sup> + 90x<sup>2</sup>, then</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The minimum value of the function <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) is 0."},{"key":"B","html":"The maximum value of the function <em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) is 53."},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has neither a maximum nor a minimum at <em>x</em> = –1."},{"key":"D","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>x</em>) has neither a maximum nor a minimum at <em>x</em> = 3."}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/39699ea71351bf043817.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider 4 processes <em>P</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>3</sub>, <em>P</em><sub>4</sub> each of which requires a certain amount of CPU time and I/O time as tabulated below:<br />\n(If the burst times are matching then follow lowest arrival time, if arrival times are matching, then follow lowest process id)</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/afc0adf7e42da4c597cf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><strong>Note:</strong> While calculating waiting time, do not consider I/O time in burst time.<br />\nIf system uses longest remaining time first algorithm, then the waiting time of process <em>P</em><sub>1</sub> is ________ units.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f93136d940b89aa5968.png\" /></div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In a 16 bit computer instruction format, the size of address field is 5 bits. The computer uses<br />\nexpanding opcode technique. It has two 2-address instructions and 1024 one address instruction.<br />\nHow many zero-address instruction can be formulated?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"28720"},{"key":"B","html":"30704"},{"key":"C","html":"30720"},{"key":"D","html":"32704"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/81/815775ecb62939d38f04.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following subnet:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/1955a316568e6646b83a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Find the link state routing table for node V using OSPF.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0b/0b733506f17777f48653.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d3/d30c2378454140507432.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/cad1cd9eb7404910f60f.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b2/b2aa5268eb001fd72ef0.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/aba53ddb2bcbc95afe43.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":55,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Two T flip-flops are interconnected as shown in the figure. The present state of the flip-flops are <em>A</em> = 1, <em>B</em> = 1. The input x is given as 1, 0, 1 in the next three clock cycles. The decimal equivalent of (<em>ABy</em>)<sub>2</sub> with A belong the MSB and y being LSB, after 3rd clock cycle is ________.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/457b2348df3f538bccac.png\" /></div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[7,7],"answerText":"7","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>7</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3a/3adca17a1720694796c6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a hash table of size 11. Let h(k) = k mode 11 be the has function used. A sequence of records with keys {33, 68, 62, 47, 48, 60, 104, 23, 19, 120, 97} is inserted into an initially empty hash table, the bins of which are indexed from zero to ten.<br />\nWhich of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"If, collisions are resolved by linear probing then maximum number of comparisons in worst case can be 2."},{"key":"B","html":"If chaining is used to resolve collision, the average chain length will be 1.09."},{"key":"C","html":"Both of these"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4c/4c11269c300fca280f67.png\" /></div>"},{"n":57,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The following C function takes a single linked list as input argument:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cd/cd6c6affa2a2a9e39f5c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The value returned by the above code is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0,0],"answerText":"0","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0</p>\n\n<p>Above code returns &lsquo;1&rsquo; if there is a cycle in a linked list.<br />\nSince the linked list which is given as an argument does not contain any cycle.<br />\nHence, value &lsquo;0&rsquo; will be returned.</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em>(<em>n</em>) denotes the number of n bit binary strings which have no pair of consecutive 1’s. Then the time complexity of the efficient algorithm which uses dynamic programming to compute <em>A</em>(<em>n</em>) will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Ο(<em>n</em>)"},{"key":"B","html":"Ο(2<em><sup>n</sup></em>)"},{"key":"C","html":"Ο(<em>n</em><sup>3</sup>)"},{"key":"D","html":"Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>)"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/68/6854eb6d612edcaf130d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":59,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following H/W design.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/47/47fd642e9520cae48274.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following are present in the address field of an instruction when the instruction is designed with register, direct and indirect addressing modes?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>r</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"2000"},{"key":"C","html":"4000"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>r</em><sub>0</sub>"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)<br />\nIn the reg AM : Addr field contain the EA<br />\nIn the direct AM : Addr field contain the EA<br />\nIn the indirect AM : Addr field contain address of an &lsquo;EA&rsquo;</p>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following scenario:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/ea1d0d3a22be4e327774.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Suppose a TCP message that contains 900 B of data and 20 B of TCP header is passed to IP code at host A for delivery to B. IP header is of 20 B.<br />\nAssume that link A-<em>R</em><sub>1</sub> has maximum frame size of 1024 B including a 14-B frame header, link <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> – <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> can support a maximum frame size of 512 B, including an 8 B frame header and link <em>R</em><sub>2</sub>-B can support a maximum frame size 512 B including a 12 B frame header.<br />\nThe length of the last fragment at each of the three links respectively are:</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"920; 440; 440"},{"key":"B","html":"960; 460; 440"},{"key":"C","html":"960; 460; 460"},{"key":"D","html":"940; 460; 460"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a3/a3baf6a3176284317bd0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a system using a segmented paging architecture. The segment is divided into 8 K pages each of size 2 K. The segment table is divided into 256 K pages each of size 512 words. The page table entry requires 64 bits and frame number requires 22 bits. Then, logical address size is ________ bits.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[51,51],"answerText":"51","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>51</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5a021812fb19d2d8f219.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/3932e86764459a8c3f24.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The minimum height of B tree index required for 9000 keys and order of B tree node (<em>P</em>) is 10 are<br />\n_________. (Assume order <em>P</em> is max possible child pointers per B tree node and root at height 0)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3,3],"answerText":"3","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/458e089487d55d6c0332.png\" /></div>"},{"n":63,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following grammar G:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bd/bd782f86bdf40b37e876.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Number of states in LR(0) parser for G are ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[8,8],"answerText":"8","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>8</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d5f7da11a0c56f1b97fe.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following relational database with the following 4 schemas and their respective instances:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c640829dea31d742480e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The number of rows returned by the above SQL query is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/88571f9d679b13af5858.png\" /></div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE & IT • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following statement(s). Then, which of the following is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The interrupting device points the processor in the direction of the proper interrupt service procedure is called as Vectored interrupt."},{"key":"B","html":"A non-maskable interrupt (NMI) is a hardware interrupt that standard interrupt-masking techniques in the system cannot ignored."},{"key":"C","html":"A non-maskable interrupt (NMI) is a hardware interrupt that standard interrupt-masking techniques in the system can ignored."},{"key":"D","html":"The normal interrupt mechanism of a microprocessor may be enabled and disabled by the programmer is called as maskable interrupt."}],"answer":["A","B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, d)<br />\n&bull; Maskable Interrupt: An Interrupt that can be disabled or ignored by the instructions of CPU are called as Maskable Interrupt.<br />\n&bull; Non-Maskable Interrupt: An interrupt that cannot be disabled or ignored by the instructions<br />\nof CPU are called as Non-Maskable Interrupt.<br />\n&bull; Vectored Interrupt: Device causing interrupt provides the starting address of Interrupt Service Routine (ISR).<br />\n&bull; Non-vectored Interrupt: Device causing interrupt does not provides the starting address of Interrupt Service Routine (ISR).</p>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":30,"NAT":20,"MSQ":15},"withoutKey":0}};
